1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,343
Aarav pulls his phone
charger out of the wall.

2
00:00:03,343 --> 00:00:07,547
But the little light on the charger
stays on for a moment, then slowly

3
00:00:07,547 --> 00:00:08,447
fades.

4
00:00:08,469 --> 00:00:09,636
The plug is out.

5
00:00:09,636 --> 00:00:12,479
So where is that light
getting its energy from?

6
00:00:12,979 --> 00:00:14,869
The answer is stored energy.

7
00:00:14,869 --> 00:00:18,717
Some parts in a circuit can
hold energy, like a tiny tank.

8
00:00:19,067 --> 00:00:21,522
Let's meet the three passive parts.

9
00:00:21,522 --> 00:00:23,924
A resistor does not store energy.

10
00:00:23,924 --> 00:00:26,539
It turns electrical energy into heat.

11
00:00:26,889 --> 00:00:31,456
A capacitor stores energy in an
electric field, between its two plates.

12
00:00:31,806 --> 00:00:36,038
And an inductor stores energy in
a magnetic field, around its coil.

13
00:00:36,688 --> 00:00:39,543
Let's watch a capacitor
fill up on the breadboard.

14
00:00:39,893 --> 00:00:44,156
Here is a battery, a capacitor, and
a small lamp, with a two way switch.

15
00:00:44,506 --> 00:00:46,379
Flip the switch to the battery.

16
00:00:46,379 --> 00:00:49,639
Electrons pile up on one plate
and leave the other plate.

17
00:00:49,989 --> 00:00:51,256
Watch the scope.

18
00:00:51,256 --> 00:00:55,974
The voltage across the capacitor rises
fast at first, then slows down and

19
00:00:55,974 --> 00:00:57,931
levels off at twelve volts.

20
00:00:58,281 --> 00:01:00,873
The charging current does the opposite.

21
00:01:00,873 --> 00:01:03,434
It starts big and dies away to zero.

22
00:01:03,434 --> 00:01:05,306
The capacitor is now full.

23
00:01:05,957 --> 00:01:07,824
Now flip the switch to the lamp.

24
00:01:07,824 --> 00:01:10,119
There is no battery in this loop at all.

25
00:01:10,469 --> 00:01:11,973
Yet the lamp lights up.

26
00:01:11,973 --> 00:01:15,607
The stored electrons rush back
around the loop through the lamp.

27
00:01:15,957 --> 00:01:20,299
On the scope, the voltage falls, and
the lamp fades out together with it.

28
00:01:20,299 --> 00:01:22,329
That is exactly the charger light.

29
00:01:22,979 --> 00:01:24,629
Resistors spend energy.

30
00:01:24,629 --> 00:01:26,978
Capacitors and inductors store it.

31
00:01:27,778 --> 00:01:30,660
So how much energy does a capacitor hold?

32
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:32,360
Let's build the formula.

33
00:01:32,710 --> 00:01:36,822
First, the charge on a capacitor
grows with its voltage.

34
00:01:36,822 --> 00:01:38,467
Q equals C times V.

35
00:01:38,467 --> 00:01:41,000
C is the capacitance, in farads.

36
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:45,943
On a graph of voltage against charge,
that is a straight line from zero.

37
00:01:46,643 --> 00:01:50,152
While charging, the voltage
climbs from zero up to V.

38
00:01:50,152 --> 00:01:54,226
So on average, each bit of charge
is pushed through V by two.

39
00:01:54,926 --> 00:01:57,295
Work equals charge times voltage.

40
00:01:57,295 --> 00:02:00,365
So the energy U equals Q times V by two.

41
00:02:00,365 --> 00:02:02,918
That is the area of this triangle.

42
00:02:03,618 --> 00:02:05,492
Now put Q equals CV.

43
00:02:05,492 --> 00:02:08,406
The energy U equals half CV squared.

44
00:02:09,106 --> 00:02:12,809
C in farads, V in volts,
and U comes out in joules.

45
00:02:13,459 --> 00:02:14,717
Worked example.

46
00:02:14,717 --> 00:02:19,155
A one hundred microfarad capacitor
is charged to twelve volts.

47
00:02:19,505 --> 00:02:24,364
U equals half, times one hundred times
ten to the minus six, times twelve

48
00:02:24,364 --> 00:02:25,264
squared.

49
00:02:25,692 --> 00:02:27,877
That is seven point two millijoules.

50
00:02:28,527 --> 00:02:31,487
Now double the voltage
to twenty four volts.

51
00:02:31,487 --> 00:02:35,174
V is squared, so the energy
becomes four times bigger.

52
00:02:35,524 --> 00:02:37,754
Twenty eight point eight millijoules.

53
00:02:37,754 --> 00:02:39,046
Look at the graph.

54
00:02:39,046 --> 00:02:41,824
The new triangle holds
four of the old ones.

55
00:02:42,474 --> 00:02:43,801
Now the inductor.

56
00:02:43,801 --> 00:02:46,049
An inductor is a coil of wire.

57
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:50,972
When current flows through it, a
magnetic field builds up around the coil.

58
00:02:50,972 --> 00:02:52,572
That field holds energy.

59
00:02:52,922 --> 00:02:55,668
Its energy is U equals half LI squared.

60
00:02:55,668 --> 00:03:00,226
L is the inductance, in henries,
and I is the current, in amperes.

61
00:03:00,876 --> 00:03:03,582
An inductor hates sudden
changes in current.

62
00:03:03,582 --> 00:03:06,242
Open the switch, and the field collapses.

63
00:03:06,592 --> 00:03:11,131
Its stored energy has to go somewhere,
so it can jump the gap as a spark.

64
00:03:11,781 --> 00:03:13,001
See the pattern?

65
00:03:13,001 --> 00:03:15,193
A capacitor holds on to voltage.

66
00:03:15,193 --> 00:03:17,384
An inductor holds on to current.

67
00:03:17,384 --> 00:03:20,042
Both formulas have a half and a square.

68
00:03:20,692 --> 00:03:22,158
Now, the exam trap.

69
00:03:22,508 --> 00:03:27,111
Many students think the capacitor
keeps all the energy the battery gives.

70
00:03:27,111 --> 00:03:28,138
It does not.

71
00:03:28,488 --> 00:03:30,463
The battery gives Q times V.

72
00:03:30,463 --> 00:03:33,706
The capacitor stores
only half of it, half QV.

73
00:03:33,706 --> 00:03:36,703
The other half is lost
as heat in the wires.

74
00:03:37,353 --> 00:03:38,929
Where is this asked?

75
00:03:38,929 --> 00:03:44,052
In PUC board papers, and in JEE Main,
for example in February twenty twenty

76
00:03:44,052 --> 00:03:46,602
one and January twenty twenty three.

77
00:03:46,602 --> 00:03:51,290
Find the energy in a capacitor or
an inductor, or the energy lost.

78
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:52,840
Try this.

79
00:03:52,834 --> 00:03:56,183
A fifty millihenry inductor
carries two amperes.

80
00:03:56,183 --> 00:04:00,098
What capacitance stores the
same energy at twenty volts?

81
00:04:05,498 --> 00:04:06,398
B.

82
00:04:06,038 --> 00:04:08,250
Five hundred microfarads.

83
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:10,673
First, the inductor energy.

84
00:04:10,673 --> 00:04:14,188
Half times zero point zero
five times two squared.

85
00:04:14,188 --> 00:04:16,443
That is zero point one joules.

86
00:04:16,793 --> 00:04:20,571
Now set half CV squared
equal to zero point one.

87
00:04:20,571 --> 00:04:23,652
So C equals two U divided by V squared.

88
00:04:24,002 --> 00:04:27,419
That is zero point two
divided by four hundred.

89
00:04:27,419 --> 00:04:32,338
Five times ten to the minus four
farads, or five hundred microfarads.

90
00:04:32,988 --> 00:04:34,512
Screenshot this card.

91
00:04:37,712 --> 00:04:40,335
Next, transducers and their applications.

92
00:04:40,335 --> 00:04:43,988
How does a thermometer turn
your body heat into a number?
